ing in an individual way was to  ensure that you were of a similar  social strata to your opponent  if they were below you they weren't  worthy of  fighting the mongols just completely  flawed them they just weren't ready  to take on an enemy that had a very  utilitarian shall we say approach  to fighting and and they weren't used to  these large bodies of men  who were able to keep their horses at  bay within  that context fighting one-on-one with  the sword will have had  similar connotations another element  that surrounds the discussion of the  katana is its metallurgical makeup  debate old and new have picked european  swords against asian weapons  arguments often encompass the pros and  cons of differing forging techniques as  well as claims that japan had  worse iron in relation to the rest of  the world and are often invoked when the  katana is compared to other swords from  across the world  the steel is extracted from an iron ore  that's kind of sluiced out of riverbeds  the raw metal that comes out of that is  very high quality  this was then smelted to produce the  swordsmith's main  starting material which is known as  tamahagany the pieces of tamahagany  would  be piled on an iron plate wrapped in wet  paper  coated in a kind of slurry and then  heated to welding temperature  the smith is aided in hammering out this  block  of metal which then famously is folded  welded and then re-hammered usually  about 15 to 20 times the effect of this  is to remove  kind of the impurities in the metal the  outcome  is to make sure the finished saw blade  has  a section of steel that is high carbon  forming  the cutting edge and the point and then  steel that is  low carbon and softer forming the main  body of the blade  the blades hammered out into the shape  with regular reheating to allow it to be  worked  the next famous stage is that it's  coated in clay the areas of the blade  that are intended to be kept soft they  have a thicker coating of clay  and the the 